# Algebraic structures

Groups and their actions, representations and characters, Lie brackets and the matrix exponential, and the facts of commutative algebra and algebraic geometry that are stated rather than computed. Permutations are written as the list of images, so $(2, 3, 1)$ sends $1 \mapsto 2$, $2 \mapsto 3$, $3 \mapsto 1$; a group is given by a list of generators. Matrix identities (rank–nullity, determinants, eigenvalues) are in [[std/linalg.mpp]]. Import with `![[std/algebra-structures.mpp]]`.

## Group theory

The symmetric group $S_3$, generated by a swap and a 3-cycle, has order 6; the swap alone generates a subgroup of order 2.

$$ S_{\text{3 gens}} \equiv ((2, 1, 3), (2, 3, 1)) $$

$$ \operatorname{grouporder}(S_{\text{3 gens}}) \overset{?}{=} 3! $$

$$ \operatorname{grouporder}(((2, 1, 3))) \overset{?}{=} 2 $$

Orbit–stabiliser: $|\operatorname{Orb}(x)| = |G|/|\operatorname{Stab}(x)|$ for a finite group action. In $S_3$ the point 1 can go anywhere and is fixed by 2 permutations.

$$ \operatorname{card}(\operatorname{orbit}(S_{\text{3 gens}}, 1)) \overset{?}{=} \frac{\operatorname{grouporder}(S_{\text{3 gens}})}{\operatorname{stabilizer}(S_{\text{3 gens}}, 1)} $$

$$ \operatorname{card}(\operatorname{orbit}(((2, 1, 3, 4), (1, 2, 4, 3)), 1)) \overset{?}{=} 2 $$

Lagrange: the order of a subgroup divides the order of a finite group.

$$ \operatorname{grouporder}(S_{\text{3 gens}}) \bmod \operatorname{grouporder}(((2, 3, 1))) \overset{?}{=} 0 $$

$$ \operatorname{grouporder}(((2, 3, 4, 1), (2, 1, 3, 4))) \bmod \operatorname{grouporder}(((2, 1, 4, 3), (3, 4, 1, 2))) \overset{?}{=} 0 $$

**Theorem.** (Orbit–stabiliser) For a finite group $G$ acting on a set, $|\operatorname{Orb}(x)| \cdot |\operatorname{Stab}(x)| = |G|$ for every point $x$.

**Theorem.** (Lagrange) The order of every subgroup of a finite group divides the order of the group.

## Representation theory

The character of a representation $\rho$ is $\chi(g) = \operatorname{tr}(\rho(g))$. The two-dimensional representation of $S_3$ sends the 3-cycle to the rotation by $2\pi/3$, whose trace $-1$ is the character value in the table of $S_3$.

$$ \chi_{\text{std}}(\theta) := \operatorname{trace}\begin{pmatrix} \cos\theta & -\sin\theta \\ \sin\theta & \cos\theta \end{pmatrix} $$

$$ \chi_{\text{std}}(\tfrac{2\pi}{3}) \overset{?}{=} -1 $$

$$ \chi_{\text{std}}(0) \overset{?}{=} 2 $$

The dimensions $d_i$ of the inequivalent complex irreducible representations of a finite group satisfy $\sum d_i^2 = |G|$: for $S_3$ they are 1, 1, 2 and for $S_4$ they are 1, 1, 2, 3, 3.

$$ 1^2 + 1^2 + 2^2 \overset{?}{=} \operatorname{grouporder}(S_{\text{3 gens}}) $$

$$ 1^2 + 1^2 + 2^2 + 3^2 + 3^2 \overset{?}{=} \operatorname{grouporder}(((2, 3, 4, 1), (2, 1, 3, 4))) $$

The full character table of $S_3$, with its class sizes:

$$ \operatorname{chartable}(S_3) = ? $$

**Theorem.** (Sum of squares) For a finite group $G$, the dimensions $d_i$ of its inequivalent complex irreducible representations satisfy $\sum_i d_i^2 = |G|$.

## Lie theory

The matrix Lie bracket $[X, Y] = XY - YX$. It is traceless, and it satisfies the Jacobi identity.

$$ X_{\text{L}} \equiv \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix} $$

$$ Y_{\text{L}} \equiv \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix} $$

$$ Z_{\text{L}} \equiv \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & -1 \end{pmatrix} $$

$$ \operatorname{liebracket}(X_{\text{L}}, Y_{\text{L}}) \overset{?}{=} Z_{\text{L}} $$

$$ \operatorname{liebracket}(X_{\text{L}}, Y_{\text{L}}) \overset{?}{=} X_{\text{L}} Y_{\text{L}} - Y_{\text{L}} X_{\text{L}} $$

$$ \operatorname{trace}(\operatorname{liebracket}(X_{\text{L}}, Z_{\text{L}})) \overset{?}{=} 0 $$

$$ \operatorname{matadd}(\operatorname{matadd}(\operatorname{liebracket}(X_{\text{L}}, \operatorname{liebracket}(Y_{\text{L}}, Z_{\text{L}})), \operatorname{liebracket}(Y_{\text{L}}, \operatorname{liebracket}(Z_{\text{L}}, X_{\text{L}}))), \operatorname{liebracket}(Z_{\text{L}}, \operatorname{liebracket}(X_{\text{L}}, Y_{\text{L}}))) \overset{?}{=} \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix} $$

The matrix exponential $\exp(tX) = \sum_{k \ge 0} (tX)^k/k!$ generates a one-parameter subgroup: $\exp(sX)\exp(tX) = \exp((s + t)X)$.

$$ \operatorname{expm}(X_{\text{L}}, t) \overset{?}{=} \begin{pmatrix} 1 & t \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} $$

$$ \operatorname{matmul}(\operatorname{expm}(X_{\text{L}}, s), \operatorname{expm}(X_{\text{L}}, t)) \overset{?}{=} \operatorname{expm}(X_{\text{L}}, s + t) $$

$$ \operatorname{trace}(\operatorname{matmul}(\operatorname{expm}(Z_{\text{L}}, 1), \operatorname{expm}(Z_{\text{L}}, 2))) \overset{?}{=} \operatorname{trace}(\operatorname{expm}(Z_{\text{L}}, 3)) $$

## Commutative algebra

**Theorem.** (Localization) For a multiplicative subset $S$ of a commutative ring $R$, the localization $S^{-1}R$ is a ring in which every $s \in S$ becomes a unit, universal among such rings. Inverting all nonzero integers in $\mathbb{Z}$ gives $\mathbb{Q}$.

$$ \frac{3}{4} \cdot \frac{4}{3} \overset{?}{=} 1 $$

**Theorem.** (Krull dimension) The Krull dimension of a commutative ring is the supremum of the lengths $n$ of chains of prime ideals $\mathfrak{p}_0 \subset \cdots \subset \mathfrak{p}_n$. For a field $k$, $\dim k[x_1, …, x_n] = n$.

## Algebraic geometry

A smooth plane projective curve of degree $d$ over an algebraically closed field has genus $(d - 1)(d - 2)/2$: lines and conics have genus 0, a smooth cubic genus 1, a quartic genus 3.

$$ g_{\text{plane}}(d) := \frac{(d - 1)(d - 2)}{2} $$

$$ g_{\text{plane}}(3) \overset{?}{=} 1 $$

$$ g_{\text{plane}}(2) \overset{?}{=} 0 $$

$$ g_{\text{plane}}(4) \overset{?}{=} 3 $$

**Theorem.** (Degree–genus formula) A smooth plane projective curve of degree $d$ over an algebraically closed field has genus $(d - 1)(d - 2)/2$.
